00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:00.630
It looks at your code,
what language it's in,

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:02.790
its third-party
dependencies, and so on,

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:05.640
then builds the most efficient
container image it can.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:07.590
Check out these links
and this video from one

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.105
of our colleagues to learn
more about Cloud Buildpacks.

00:01:11.105 --> 00:01:12.480
MARTIN OMANDER:
That's good info.

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.803
But I'm curious, where did
these Buildpacks come from?

00:01:15.803 --> 00:01:17.970
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah, well,
they originated from Heroku

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:19.440
back in 2011.

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:21.270
And since then,
Buildpacks took off

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:23.490
for different languages,
different cloud vendors.

00:01:23.490 --> 00:01:26.910
But in 2018, the Cloud Native
Computing Foundation unified

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.950
Buildpacks under
a common standard.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:31.710
Google Cloud Buildpacks
are 100% compatible

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:35.280
with the Cloud Native Buildpacks
and adheres to its open spec.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:38.880
Check out the links to the Cloud
Native Buildpacks and its spec.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:40.740
Cloud Buildpacks
containerizes apps

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.320
to be deployed on Google
Cloud, whether Cloud Run,

00:01:43.320 --> 00:01:45.660
GKE, which is our managed
Kubernetes service,

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:48.277
or Compute Engine VMs.

00:01:48.277 --> 00:01:49.610
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, sounds good.

00:01:49.610 --> 00:01:51.370
I can't wait to try it.

00:01:51.370 --> 00:01:54.140
So I did look ahead
and I found out

00:01:54.140 --> 00:01:58.510
there's one module 5 finish
folder, which is Python 3.

00:01:58.510 --> 00:02:00.517
What happened to Python 2, Wes?

00:02:00.517 --> 00:02:02.350
WESLEY CHUN: Well,
unfortunately, Buildpacks

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:03.790
don't support Python 2.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:06.250
So if you do have a Python 2
app, you got to use Docker.

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.190
But so our starting point
will be the Python 3 version

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.230
of the Maldo 2 Cloud NDB app.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.960
We didn't containerize that
one with Docker last time,

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.670
so let's pause right
now so all of you

00:02:15.670 --> 00:02:18.490
can go grab the code from
the module 2b folder,

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:21.430
or use your own from
the module 2 migration.

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:22.210
Got it?

00:02:22.210 --> 00:02:23.980
You can also follow
along in the codelab

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:26.080
and get hands-on experience
containerizing it

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:28.940
with Cloud Buildpacks.

00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:32.030
Let's start by confirming
the module 2 code works.

00:02:32.030 --> 00:02:35.810
Whether using your app or ours,
redeploy with gcloud app deploy

00:02:35.810 --> 00:02:37.550
and verify you get
the same output

00:02:37.550 --> 00:02:39.200
as from previous modules.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:41.180
If you've done any of
these migration modules,

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:43.610
you know that this app
typically doesn't change at all.

00:02:43.610 --> 00:02:44.750
That's the purpose, right?

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:47.150
Migrating without
breaking anything?

00:02:47.150 --> 00:02:49.610
Just like in module 4,
we're switching products

00:02:49.610 --> 00:02:51.650
from App Engine to
Cloud Run, so make

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.050
a full copy of your
module2 app folder,

00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:56.810
because like with Docker,
you're going to delete app.yaml,

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:58.880
as it's not used in Cloud Run.

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.360
Next, let's tackle
requirements.txt.

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:03.200
Quick review.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.500
App Engine and Cloud
Functions are source-based

00:03:06.500 --> 00:03:09.290
and start your app with a
production-ready server.

00:03:09.290 --> 00:03:12.050
Since Cloud Run is a
little bit more DIY,

00:03:12.050 --> 00:03:13.790
you have to roll your own.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:17.670
Python App Engine uses gunicorn
if you don't specify your own,

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.730
so we're going to use
that with Cloud Run

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:22.190
by adding it to
requirements.txt,

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:24.950
so it's identical to the
one in module2b folder

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.440
except with gunicorn
at the top, since I

00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.320
like to put mine in lowest
to highest level order.

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:32.630
Yes, version numbers
are a good practice.

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:35.280
I left them out here because
they change all the time.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.150
However, they will be
updated in the repo.

00:03:38.150 --> 00:03:40.760
Like with Docker, the app
itself doesn't change.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:43.100
We're just
containerizing it, right?

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:45.620
The last thing needed
is to launch gunicorn

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:46.820
to start our app.

00:03:46.820 --> 00:03:49.580
With Docker, there's either
a command or entry point

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:51.350
directive at the end
of the Docker file

00:03:51.350 --> 00:03:53.030
to kick off gunicorn.

00:03:53.030 --> 00:03:56.780
With Cloud Buildpacks, there
is no app.yaml nor Dockerfile.

00:03:56.780 --> 00:03:59.850
Instead, we use a
Heroku-style Procfile.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.930
It's basically a config file
describing your app's processes

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:04.430
and how to start them.

00:04:04.430 --> 00:04:07.820
For our app, there's only
one process, the web server.

00:04:07.820 --> 00:04:10.970
It's basically the entry
point directive from app.yaml

00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:12.350
or the Dockerfile.

00:04:12.350 --> 00:04:15.620
So create yours with this
one line specifying gunicorn

00:04:15.620 --> 00:04:16.880
as our web process.

00:04:16.880 --> 00:04:19.142
And boom, that is it.

00:04:19.142 --> 00:04:21.350
We're also going to drop
some links down below so you

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:23.480
can learn more about Procfiles.

00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:26.180
And that, we say, is a wrap.

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.204
Run the same gcloud
run deploy SVC_NAME --

00:04:29.204 --> 00:04:29.704
.

00:04:29.704 --> 00:04:33.050
Command as you did with
Docker, or the longer version,

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:35.240
with the platform type,
region, and so on,

00:04:35.240 --> 00:04:36.470
if you're lazy like me.

00:04:36.470 --> 00:04:38.570
And in about a minute
or less, your app

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.210
will be available
globally on Cloud Run.

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:43.850
It should work the same as
before, whether on App Engine

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:45.710
or Cloud Run with Docker.

00:04:45.710 --> 00:04:48.590
Now you've containerized
your App Engine app again,

00:04:48.590 --> 00:04:50.600
but this time,
without a Dockerfile.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.337
Congratulations.

00:04:53.337 --> 00:04:54.170
MARTIN OMANDER: Wow.

00:04:54.170 --> 00:04:55.690
I didn't know
creating and deploying

00:04:55.690 --> 00:04:57.835
a container would be that easy.

00:04:57.835 --> 00:05:00.010
Buildpacks are amazing.

00:05:00.010 --> 00:05:02.380
Since I don't have to worry
about Dockerfiles anymore,

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:05.560
I'm thinking about how to
increase my app's presence.

00:05:05.560 --> 00:05:08.500
While my users here in the
US don't have any issues,

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:12.070
my friends in Sweden are
saying it's sometimes laggy.

00:05:12.070 --> 00:05:14.973
Can I deploy my container
to other regions?

00:05:14.973 --> 00:05:16.390
WESLEY CHUN: Oh,
for sure, Martin.

00:05:16.390 --> 00:05:19.300
In these past few videos,
we used the magic one-line

00:05:19.300 --> 00:05:22.210
build and deploy command to
get you started with the least

00:05:22.210 --> 00:05:23.110
friction.

00:05:23.110 --> 00:05:26.050
That's gcloud run
deploy with --source

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:28.120
to specify the code to deploy.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:31.390
For more sophisticated usage,
let's talk about breaking it up

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:33.190
into two separate
commands that'll

00:05:33.190 --> 00:05:35.440
make it more flexible
to do the other things,

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:37.490
like what you asked.

00:05:37.490 --> 00:05:38.960
MARTIN OMANDER:
Sounds good, Wes.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:41.990
I'm willing to give up some
convenience so I can more

00:05:41.990 --> 00:05:44.900
easily deploy my app elsewhere.

00:05:44.900 --> 00:05:48.470
But you said that one-line
command builds and deploys

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:49.760
my container.

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:51.800
Can I guess that the
two separate commands

00:05:51.800 --> 00:05:54.890
are one for the build and
the other for the deploy?

00:05:54.890 --> 00:05:56.090
WESLEY CHUN: Precisely.

00:05:56.090 --> 00:05:58.310
With the one-line
command, we got your app

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:00.920
from code all the
way to Cloud Run

00:06:00.920 --> 00:06:03.740
without discussing container
images or other resources,

00:06:03.740 --> 00:06:06.830
like Cloud Build, Container
Registry, or its successor,

00:06:06.830 --> 00:06:08.180
Artifact Registry.

00:06:08.180 --> 00:06:10.200
But since the cat's
out of the bag,

00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:14.060
here's the gcloud build
submit --tag command.

00:06:14.060 --> 00:06:16.040
You provide the full
image path, which

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:17.720
is made up of the
registry namespace,

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.590
like gcr.io or package.dev,
respectively, your project

00:06:21.590 --> 00:06:24.440
ID, image name, and
possibly the region.

00:06:24.440 --> 00:06:25.820
When you build
your image, it has

00:06:25.820 --> 00:06:28.670
to be registered before you
can deploy to Cloud Run.

00:06:28.670 --> 00:06:31.940
Registries help manage images,
provide access control,

00:06:31.940 --> 00:06:33.620
lets you check for
vulnerabilities,

00:06:33.620 --> 00:06:36.350
and is where you plug
into CI/CD integrations.

00:06:36.350 --> 00:06:37.790
Check out the
links to the gcloud

00:06:37.790 --> 00:06:41.300
build submit command, as well as
both registries, to learn more.

00:06:41.300 --> 00:06:44.630
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, so that's
the build and register step.

00:06:44.630 --> 00:06:46.940
Now how do I deploy the
same container image

00:06:46.940 --> 00:06:48.405
to multiple regions?

00:06:48.405 --> 00:06:49.280
WESLEY CHUN: Hold on.

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:50.750
We're getting there, Martin.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:52.520
Now that the image
is in the registry,

00:06:52.520 --> 00:06:54.200
it's straightforward to deploy.

00:06:54.200 --> 00:06:57.260
But instead of gcloud
run deploy --source,

00:06:57.260 --> 00:06:59.720
it's gcloud run
deploy --image, where

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:01.550
you provide the image pathname.

00:07:01.550 --> 00:07:03.890
Using this command, you
can deploy the same image

00:07:03.890 --> 00:07:07.050
to other regions without having
to build identical containers.

00:07:07.050 --> 00:07:09.380
Another benefit to having
your image in the registry

00:07:09.380 --> 00:07:11.330
is that you can launch
services directly

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:14.900
from the Cloud Console whenever
you want from wherever you are.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:17.180
In other words, you don't
have to be on a command line

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:18.080
at your terminal.

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:21.262
You can be in Sweden to
launch that service there.

00:07:21.262 --> 00:07:23.720
MARTIN OMANDER: OK, I feel good
about containerizing my App

00:07:23.720 --> 00:07:25.760
Engine app for Cloud Run.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:28.550
What are some other App
Engine migrations to consider?

00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:30.800
WESLEY CHUN: Yeah, well,
where you usually go usually

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:33.080
depends on the
services you're using.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:35.150
Apps using Cloud
NDB may consider

00:07:35.150 --> 00:07:36.980
migrating to Cloud
Datastore if you have

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:38.600
other code using that library.

00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:40.790
If so, take a look at module 3.

00:07:40.790 --> 00:07:42.800
But if all you have are
apps using Cloud NDB,

00:07:42.800 --> 00:07:44.300
just stay where you are.

00:07:44.300 --> 00:07:46.550
If you're excited about the
Firebase features in Cloud

00:07:46.550 --> 00:07:50.630
Firestore, you can consider that
migration in modules 6 and 10.

00:07:50.630 --> 00:07:52.640
If your app uses App
Engine task queues,

00:07:52.640 --> 00:07:54.770
consider migrating
to Cloud Tasks.

00:07:54.770 --> 00:07:57.980
You can learn more about
those in modules 7, 8, and 9.

00:07:57.980 --> 00:08:00.350
Finally, if your app only
has one basic function

00:08:00.350 --> 00:08:02.820
or if you're interested in
breaking up a larger App Engine

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.990
app into a set of microservices,
check out module 11

00:08:05.990 --> 00:08:07.995
to learn about migrating
to Cloud Functions.

00:08:07.995 --> 00:08:08.870
MARTIN OMANDER: Whoa.

00:08:08.870 --> 00:08:11.010
That's a lot of options.

